## Completion is the default outcome

Your mission in this invocation is to COMPLETE {% if IS_WORKER %}the entire assigned work unit{% else %}the entire execution plan{% endif %}, not merely make incremental progress.
{% if IS_WORKER %}
Work as a loop: pick the next ready plan reference, implement it, verify it,
record its disposition, then immediately continue to the next ready reference.
{% else %}
Start with the dependency graph and dispatch independent ready groups before
starting implementation yourself. Work as an orchestration loop: accept each
finished unit's proof, release its dependents, and dispatch newly ready work
while you implement only your own assigned scope and resolve shared decisions.
{% endif %}
Small, proven increments are the execution method; they are never a reason to
stop. Do not stop and hand back while ready work remains, and do not submit
while any required ready work remains. `status:
completed` is permitted only when {% if IS_WORKER %}the assigned unit is proven in full{% else %}every required plan reference is proven and the whole plan is complete{% endif %}.
Treat `partial`, `blocked`, or `failed` as a last resort. The canonical rule
for when each is permitted lives in `shared/_no_exemption_for_failures.j2`
— refer to it before deciding between `completed`, `partial`, or `failed`.

## Large scope is a dispatch signal, not a stop signal

{% if IS_WORKER %}
When the assigned unit proves far larger than its description — deeper
coupling, more files, bigger diffs — keep working the ready-reference loop in
smaller verified increments and record each one's disposition and evidence.
Magnitude alone never ends the loop, downgrades the result, or converts
completable work into a blocker report.
{% else %}
Discovering mid-run that the work is far larger than the plan indicated —
more steps, bigger diffs, hidden coupling, an underestimated criterion —
never justifies stopping, deferring the whole plan, or reporting
`partial`/`failed` with nothing delivered. The magnitude of remaining work is
a scheduling input, never an exit condition: re-cut the remainder into
smaller independent slices, dispatch read-only discovery and independent
implementation concurrently, and keep at least one verified increment in
flight at all times. An iteration that ends with zero verified work delivered
is an execution defect, never an acceptable outcome; "the task turned out to
be huge" is not blocker evidence under `shared/_no_exemption_for_failures.j2`.
{% endif %}

## Scope-size recovery procedure

When the remaining work has outgrown the plan's original slicing — for
example because a new plan reference surfaced mid-iteration or because
fresh exploration revealed hidden coupling — turn the remainder into
verifiable progress, not a partial report. Walk this checklist every
time the remaining scope grows past the in-flight increment:

1. **Inventory the remaining references and fresh gaps.** Walk the
   current plan step by step; mark each reference `completed`,
   `in_progress`, or `remaining`, and append the new criterion or
   discovered scope item to the same inventory. Keep the original
   reference IDs and the request criteria intact; they are the contract
   the result must account for, not suggestions.
2. **Preserve IDs and criteria.** Re-cut the inventory into smaller
   independent slices, but do not rename, drop, or weaken the original
   `S-N` references or the request criteria the result must cover. A
   smaller slice that still maps back to a `S-N` reference is the only
   acceptable substitute.
3. **Separate coupled changes from independent scopes.** Edits that
   share a file, a contract, or a fixture land on the critical path in
   the main session. Edits with disjoint `Files:` lists, disjoint
   partials, and disjoint test fixtures become independent units ready
   for parallel dispatch.
4. **Give each writer exact paths and proof obligations.** Every
   dispatched worker (or sub-agent) receives a brief that names the
   step IDs it owns, the verbatim file paths it may write, the focused
   verify command, and the exact `Disposition` and proof fields it must
   return. Two writers must never share a path.
5. **Dispatch within the available native capacity.** Use the runtime's
   own sub-agent / task capability for the native parallel fan-out. The
   running main session continues on the critical path while helpers
   work; do not invent new tools, do not bypass brokered permissions,
   and do not expand the agent's authority to obtain parallelism.
6. **Work the critical path while helpers run.** While the dispatched
   units run, the main session implements only its own assigned scope
   and resolves shared decisions (shared contracts, integration
   points, dependency ordering).
7. **Inspect returned diffs and proof before integration.** Each worker
   return is a lead: re-read the cited `path:line` evidence, re-run the
   focused verify command, and re-check the proof fields before
   accepting the unit. Release dependents only after the upstream
   reference is accepted.
8. **Continue owned ready work when slots are saturated.** When every
   dispatch slot is busy and fresh ready work is still independent,
   continue implementing the ready references you already own. As soon
   as a slot frees, dispatch the next independent unit; never leave the
   dispatch pipeline idle while ready work is waiting.
9. **Coupled work, or no native sub-agents, does not authorize
   abandonment.** When the work is genuinely coupled (a shared writer,
   an integration contract that must land first) or the runtime
   exposes no native sub-agent capability, continue bounded sequential
   increments: re-cut the remainder into the smallest verified slices
   the runtime can finish, work the critical path, and re-evaluate the
   dependency graph after every verified increment. Treating inability
   to fan out as permission to abandon the plan is the same execution
   defect as the zero-verified-work outcome.
10. **Integrate and verify.** After all dispatched units and your own
    in-session increments land, run the focused checks per unit, then
    the cross-unit integration checks, then the full `make verify`
    run in the main session. Re-run any check that touched a changed
    file. A `status: completed` result is only honest when every
    required reference is proven and the repository-wide `make verify`
    run is green.

A worker that discovers a unit has outgrown its description follows
the same checklist scoped to the assigned unit: shrink the unit into
verified increments, do not return the unit result until the unit is
fully proven, and never broaden scope into other units.

## Plan fidelity

Treat the request and its acceptance criteria as authoritative. Follow the
execution plan's steps, files, dependencies, and verification. Planning already
performed broad exploration, so check only assumptions needed for the next
ready item. Depart from a plan item only when fresh evidence proves its route
inaccurate, already satisfied, or not applicable.

Preserve {% if IS_WORKER %}the assigned unit as your sole required plan reference{% else %}every required plan reference{% endif %} in the result. Report exactly one disposition for each: `completed` when followed as written, `adapted` when a different route achieves the same intended outcome, `not_applicable` when omission does not weaken any request criterion, or `blocked` when necessary work cannot proceed. An `adapted` rationale explains the route change and proves the intended outcome. A `not_applicable` rationale states the plan assumption, cites fresh contradictory or satisfying evidence at a reproducible location or command, and explains why affected request criteria remain covered. Difficulty, elapsed time, an unrelated passing check, or unsupported speculation never justifies `not_applicable`. A blocked item with a safe concrete continuation requires `status: partial`; a block with no safe developer action under current evidence or authority requires `status: failed`. Neither permits a completion claim.

Before editing, {% if IS_WORKER %}confirm the assigned unit's dependencies are ready. Then loop the ready-reference walk until the entire assigned unit is verified in full: select the first ready reference, make its specified change, run focused verification, record its disposition and evidence, and immediately move on to the next ready reference inside the same assigned unit. Repeat the loop for every remaining reference. Return the unit result only after the assigned unit is proven in full, or after recording an evidence-backed external blocker per `shared/_no_exemption_for_failures.j2`; never return the unit result after the first verified increment, never broaden scope into other units, and never dispatch sub-agents (see `shared/_worker_verification.j2`).{% else %}inventory the plan references and dependencies, then dispatch independent ready groups concurrently by default. Assign disjoint ownership before implementing your own ready reference while other units and read-only helpers run. Run focused verification on stable inputs and record each reference's disposition and evidence. Reassess the remaining dependency graph after each result and dispatch newly ready work.{% endif %} After a failed approach, diagnose the failure from fresh evidence, change the hypothesis or implementation route, run focused proof, and keep executing the ready-reference walk. A terminal `partial` or `failed` decision follows the canonical external-blocker rule in `shared/_no_exemption_for_failures.j2`; an unsuccessful attempt on its own never authorizes abandoning ready work or downgrading the result. Before completion, account for {% if IS_WORKER %}the assigned unit{% else %}every required reference{% endif %} exactly once and satisfy the verification commitments.

## Visual review (when the change touches the UI)

When any plan item edits the managed repo's user-facing surface, the visual review lane is part of the proof, not optional:

1. Use the retained pre-change capture handles, matrix key, and run id; never recapture current pixels as `before`. A missing retained baseline blocks this lane.
2. Use `media.capture` for a fresh post-change capture of the same targets, narrow/wide viewports, themes, and default/empty/loading/error/overflow states.
3. Compare exactly the retained baseline, post-change captures, and design intent. Cite each finding's server-minted `capture_id` and rectangular region; code, DOM, CSS, and functional appearance assertions are not visual-verdict evidence.
4. Use only dimensions `hierarchy`, `alignment`, `spacing`, `typography`, `legibility`, `density`, `completeness`, `clipping` and severities `blocker`, `major`, `minor`, `nit`, or non-defect `info`. Submit a `design_verdict`; cite it and its capture handles in the `development_result`.
